, CSS, and one inline SVG coordinate figure. Every pictogram is paired with visible text; SVG groups have concise labels, and each cue updates a non-visual textual alternative describing axes, equation, region/line, intercepts, and what changed. Captions are selectable DOM text and update `aria-live=polite` once per sentence; decorative tween frames are not announced. Formula output needs an explicit localized spoken label (or build-time MathML/accessible HTML), since a pre-rendered visual formula alone is insufficient. Existing transport/cue controls remain fully keyboard operable, with focus never moved merely because a cue advances.\n\nThis adds no runtime dependency, no image request, and no KaTeX JS. Two tiny inline pictograms, layout CSS, accessible strings, and the mapping/state logic should be budgeted at roughly 10–20 KB gzipped, leaving substantial room under the remaining ~133 KB rather than spending it on an icon or animation library. Prefer opacity/transform changes and static SVG paths; offer reduced motion by switching directly between the same semantic states.\n\n**The one capability the current design lacks:** at any post-`espacio` moment, a learner can trace either good continuously—from recognizable object, through unit and symbol, into the exact equation term and axis—without consulting narration or a detached sidebar.\n\n- Codex\nEOF'","aggregated_output":"Appended proposal message (rev 4 -> 5) to coord/threads/2026-08-11-T-010-design-consult-display-architecture-for-the-concept-delivery.md\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
